const article: BlogArticle = {"slug": "ai-character-generator", "oldSlugs": [], "column": "guide", "category": "guide", "title": "AI Character Generator: Stop Face Drift Across Shots", "seoTitle": "AI Character Generator: Stop Face Drift | DramaCanvas", "summary": "To run an AI character generator that preserves the same face across shots, connect up to 30 reference images into a video node on an infinite canvas. Learn how to generate a four-view character sheet, lock your wardrobe prompt, and wire character cards to video and audio nodes.", "author": "", "date": "2026-11-04", "modifiedDate": "2026-11-04", "updated": "2026-11-04", "status": "published", "seoApproved": true, "sourceLocale": "en-US", "publishedLocales": ["en-US"], "intro": ["An **ai character generator** workflow solves the single hardest obstacle in generative filmmaking: preventing a character's face from morphing between shots. When you prompt a video model from text alone, the system predicts a completely new human being for every generation. Reliable narrative filmmaking requires locking that identity in still images first and passing those visuals directly into video generation.", "On an infinite canvas, you accomplish this by building a multi-angle master character sheet, saving it to your project, and wiring those reference stills directly into downstream video and audio nodes. By combining image models such as [[link:gpt-image-guide-2026|GPT Image 2]] or [[link:nano-banana-pro|Nano Banana Pro]] with multi-reference video generators like [[link:seedance-2-5|Seedance 2.5]], you can maintain the same facial features, build, and wardrobe across an entire series.", "Reading this guide takes about 14 minutes. By the end, you will have a copyable prompting framework, a model selection strategy, and a node-by-node pipeline for rendering the same character in every shot on [DramaCanvas](https://dramacanva.com)."], "takeaways": ["**Master sheets anchor visual identity**: Generating a four-view reference sheet with front, three-quarter, profile, and full-body angles establishes facial structure and proportions before rendering motion.", "**Reference capacity dictates consistency**: ByteDance's [[link:seedance-2-5|Seedance 2.5]] accepts up to 30 reference images simultaneously, providing the highest visual retention among models available on DramaCanvas.", "**Story instructions override static sheets**: An identity reference card locks facial features and bone structure, while your scene prompt controls dynamic actions, expressions, and environmental weathering.", "**Visual wiring costs zero credits**: Adding image cards, cropping angles, and connecting reference lines across the infinite canvas deducts no balance until you submit a video task for rendering."], "questions": [{"id": "question-1", "title": "How does an ai character generator keep the same character in every shot?"}, {"id": "question-2", "title": "How do you design and prompt a master character sheet?"}, {"id": "question-3", "title": "Which video model provides the best character consistency on DramaCanvas?"}, {"id": "question-4", "title": "How do you wire character references into video and audio nodes on the canvas?"}, {"id": "question-5", "title": "What causes character drift in AI video, and how do you fix it?"}], "sections": [{"id": "question-1", "title": "How does an ai character generator keep the same character in every shot?", "paragraphs": ["An ai character generator preserves identity by conditioning downstream video generation on 4 to 30 approved reference stills rather than text prompts alone. When you provide only text, the model invents new facial features, hairstyles, and bone structures each time you click generate. Supplying reference images steers the model toward the same face, proportions and outfit in every shot.", "As documented in [ScreenWeaver's character reference course](https://www.screenweaver.ai/academy/courses/ai-filmmaking-course/consistent-ai-characters-reference-sheets), human audiences detect facial inconsistency immediately, which breaks narrative immersion. To overcome this, professional pipelines separate identity from shot variables. Your reference images define facial anatomy and wardrobe silhouettes, while your text prompt dictates camera movement, emotional performance, and scene lighting.", "On DramaCanvas, this separation is physical. You draft your script in a Text node, render reference stills in an Image node, and route those images through data wires into a Video node. The downstream model reads the connected pixels as the look of your actor, which greatly reduces facial drift across cuts."], "blocks": [{"type": "paragraph", "index": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 1}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 2}]}, {"id": "question-2", "title": "How do you design and prompt a master character sheet?", "paragraphs": ["A master character sheet requires 4 core views—front portrait, three-quarter angle, side profile, and full-body silhouette—rendered against a flat grey studio background. Designing this sheet in an image model first gives your downstream video models complete spatial information about your character's jawline, hairline, and body shape.", "According to [Astorie's character consistency workflow](https://astorie.ai/how-to/keep-same-character-across-ai-videos), generating four distinct angles on a single master sheet prevents the facial drifting that occurs when you generate individual reference portraits separately. Image models such as [[link:gpt-image-guide-2026|GPT Image 2]] and [[link:nano-banana-pro|Nano Banana Pro]] excel at producing clean multi-panel contact sheets when given explicit layout constraints.", "**Step 1: Write an identity paragraph and character block**", "Start by defining your character's visual baseline in a Text node on the canvas. Describe age, ethnic background, face shape, hair texture, eye shape, and exactly two distinctive signature items, such as a knit mustard cardigan or a faint scar over the left eyebrow. Research from [SuperDuperAI's reference sheet guide](https://superduperai.co/library/character-consistency-reference-sheet/) shows that two prominent visual signatures anchor identity far more effectively than ten vague adjectives.", "Never change this identity paragraph between scenes. Paste this exact description block into every downstream prompt to reinforce the visual references.", "**Step 2: Generate the multi-angle sheet in an Image node**", "Click **Add node** on the bottom toolbar of the canvas, select **Image**, and set the aspect ratio to 16:9 in image settings. Paste your identity block combined with explicit multi-panel layout instructions into the prompt box.", "Click **Generate** to render the sheet. Once completed, switch the canvas to Professional mode, hover over the Image card's toolbar, and select **Grid split** to slice the contact sheet into standalone child cards. This gives you distinct cards for front, profile, and three-quarter angles without running extra generations."], "blocks": [{"type": "paragraph", "index": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 1}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 2}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 3}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 4}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 5}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 6}, {"type": "code", "index": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 7}, {"type": "figure", "index": 0}], "code": [{"label": "Master character sheet prompt", "content": "Character reference sheet of Marcus: a 28-year-old man, sharp jawline, short textured dark fade haircut, amber-brown eyes, and a small faint scar over his left eyebrow. Athletic build, wearing a clean olive utility jacket over a charcoal crewneck t-shirt and dark denim. Four distinct views arranged horizontally: full-body front-facing standing pose, head-and-shoulders three-quarter portrait, sharp side profile, and full-body rear view showing hairstyle and jacket back. Neutral facial expression, even flat studio lighting, solid neutral light grey background, clean concept art layout. No text, no overlapping figures, perfectly consistent facial structure and wardrobe across all four panels.", "language": "text"}], "figures": [{"src": "/blog-media/figures/ai-character-generator/fig-01-sheet.jpg", "webp": "/blog-media/figures/ai-character-generator/fig-01-sheet.webp", "width": 1920, "height": 1412, "alt": "Four-view character master sheet for Marcus with prompt before Grid split", "caption": "Four-view character master sheet for Marcus with prompt before Grid split"}]}, {"id": "question-3", "title": "Which video model provides the best character consistency on DramaCanvas?", "paragraphs": ["ByteDance's Seedance 2.5 supports up to 30 reference images simultaneously, making it the most flexible video model for character retention on DramaCanvas. When choosing a video generator, your primary metric is reference capacity: models that accept multiple image inputs maintain facial fidelity through complex head turns and camera rotations far better than single-frame models.", "Since OpenAI shut down the Sora 2 models and Videos API on September 24, 2026, multi-reference models are the main way to keep a character consistent. You can explore full platform benchmarks in our guide to [[link:sora-shutdown-alternatives|Sora alternatives]] and our ranking of the [[link:best-ai-video-generator-2026|best AI video generators in 2026]].", "The table below breaks down the video models available on DramaCanvas, their credit costs, and their input limits for character consistency:", "| Model (maker) | Resolution & credits per second (credits/s) | Length (s) | Aspect ratios | Max reference images | Limitations & Input Rules | |---|---|---|---|---|---| | Seedance 2.5 (ByteDance) | 480p: 14 · 720p: 30 · 1080p: 74 | 4–30 s | 6 ratios | Up to 30 images | Up to 10 reference audio clips; rejects reference video on DramaCanvas | | Seedance 2.0 (ByteDance) | 480p: 10 · 720p: 20 · 1080p: 50 | 4–15 s (720p to 30 s) | 6 ratios | Up to 9 images | Supports 3 reference videos and 3 reference audio clips | | Seedance 2.0 Fast | 480p: 8 · 720p: 17 | 4–15 s | 6 ratios | Up to 9 images | Lower latency; supports 3 reference videos and 3 audio clips | | Seedance 2.0 Mini | 480p: 7 · 720p: 10 | 4–15 s | 6 ratios | Up to 9 images | Cost-effective; no reference video input allowed | | Wan 3.0 (Alibaba) | 480p: 6 · 720p: 12 · 1080p: 24 | 2–30 s | 6 ratios | Up to 10 images | Also takes 5 reference videos and 5 audio clips | | Kling 4 (Kuaishou) | 720p: 7 · 1080p: 12 | 3–30 s | 16:9 / 9:16 / 1:1 / 21:9 | Up to 10 images | Lowest 720p rate here; images only, no video or audio references | | Kling 3.0 (Kuaishou) | 720p: 8 (12 with audio) · 1080p: 16 · 4K: 40 | 3–15 s | 16:9 / 9:16 / 1:1 | 2 images max | Operates strictly as start frame and end frame; no general reference tray | | MiniMax H3 (Hailuo) | 480p: 5 · 768p: 8 · 1080p: 13 | 4–30 s (1080p to 15 s) | 6 ratios | 1 image max | Operates strictly as start frame; rejects multi-image reference wiring | | Grok Imagine Video 1.5 (xAI) | 480p: 6 · 720p: 10 | 4–15 s | 6 ratios | Up to 7 images | Up to 720p; 15-second limit |", "Credits checked on DramaCanvas on 2026-11-04.", "DramaCanvas calculates billing per second of generated video. On the Standard plan ($29.90 per month for 1,100 credits), 1 credit equals approximately $0.027. Failed generations are refunded to your account balance automatically.", "To evaluate actual production costs:", "For narrative character consistency, choose Seedance 2.5 when you need to feed comprehensive wardrobe, facial, and environment stills into a single generation. When working on tighter production budgets, Kling 4 and Wan 3.0 provide high visual fidelity across up to 10 reference images at lower credit rates."], "blocks": [{"type": "paragraph", "index": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 1}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 2}, {"type": "table", "index": 3}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 4}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 5}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 6}, {"type": "list", "items": ["[[link:seedance-2-5|Seedance 2.5]] at 720p for a 10-second shot consumes 300 credits ($8.10).", "[[link:seedance-2|Seedance 2.0]] at 720p for a 10-second shot consumes 200 credits ($5.40).", "[[link:wan-3-guide|Wan 3.0]] at 720p for a 10-second shot consumes 120 credits ($3.24).", "[[link:kling-ai-guide-2026|Kling 4]] at 720p for a 10-second shot consumes 70 credits ($1.89)."]}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 7}]}, {"id": "question-4", "title": "How do you wire character references into video and audio nodes on the canvas?", "paragraphs": ["On DramaCanvas, connecting character consistency references means dragging a line from the right output port of an Image node to the left input port of a Video or Audio card. The visual canvas structure replaces complex prompt engineering with direct spatial routing, allowing one master character sheet to feed dozens of scene cards simultaneously.", "As outlined in [Fawna's character prompting documentation](https://fawna.ai/docs/prompting/character/), video models respond strongly to visual references paired with exact verbal descriptions. On DramaCanvas, wires carry media from left to right.", "**Step 3: Save cropped portraits as character cards in project assets**", "In the top bar, ensure your canvas is linked to a project under \"My canvases.\" Grouping episode boards under a project lets you access the \"Project assets\" drawer.", "Select your approved front-facing portrait card and profile card from the Grid split step. Add them to Project assets as a named character card (for example, \"Marcus\"). Saving character assets at the project level allows multiple episode canvases to inherit the same reference cards without uploading them repeatedly.", "**Step 4: Wire reference images and assign start frames in the Video node**", "Click **Add node** on the bottom toolbar, select **Video**, and place it to the right of your image cards. Select your model, such as [[link:kling-ai-guide-2026|Kling 4]] or [[link:seedance-2-5|Seedance 2.5]].", "Click and hold the circular output handle on the right edge of your character card, drag the preview wire across the board, and release it over the left input port of the Video node. Repeat this process for your three-quarter and full-body cards. DramaCanvas automatically deduplicates connections: wiring the same source image multiple times consumes only one reference slot.", "Under the prompt input box, expand **Parameters**. In the **Start frame** dropdown, select your keyframe still to anchor the opening composition of the shot. If your shot requires general reference likeness rather than a frozen starting point, leave Start frame set to \"Not specified\"; the model will use the connected cards purely as identity anchors.", "**Step 5: Write the scene shot prompt with @Image binding**", "Type your scene prompt using a structured five-part formula: Setting + Character Appearance + Core Action + Dialogue + Camera Trajectory. Reference your wired image explicitly using `@Image` notation.", "Check the credit cost displayed beside the generate arrow, then click **Generate**. The task advances through Queued and Generating states, rendering an MP4 video clip directly on the card.", "**Step 6: Connect a voice-bound character card to an Audio node**", "Visual consistency must be paired with vocal continuity. Click **Add node** in the bottom dock and select **Audio** (measuring 340×120).", "Under parameters, Voice defaults to alloy alongside alternative vocal profiles, format defaults to mp3, and speech rate toggles between 0.75x and 1.5x with a slider spanning 0.25x to 4.0x. In \"Voice instructions,\" describe your character's cadence in plain language (such as \"A cautious, low-pitched male voice speaking in a quiet, measured tempo\").", "Connect your character card to the Audio node's left input port. Note the strict connection rule: Audio nodes accept only text prompts or exactly 1 voice-bound character card. They reject incoming video clips, raw image stills, or auxiliary audio files."], "blocks": [{"type": "paragraph", "index": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 1}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 2}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 3}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 4}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 5}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 6}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 7}, {"type": "figure", "index": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 8}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 9}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 10}, {"type": "code", "index": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 11}, {"type": "figure", "index": 1}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 12}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 13}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 14}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 15}], "figures": [{"src": "/blog-media/figures/ai-character-generator/fig-02-references.jpg", "webp": "/blog-media/figures/ai-character-generator/fig-02-references.webp", "width": 1920, "height": 2368, "alt": "Wiring the character master card to the subway shot Video node input port", "caption": "Wiring the character master card to the subway shot Video node input port"}, {"src": "/blog-media/figures/ai-character-generator/fig-03-settings.jpg", "webp": "/blog-media/figures/ai-character-generator/fig-03-settings.webp", "width": 1920, "height": 2978, "alt": "Seedance 2.5 at 720P, 9:16 and 10s showing 300 credits on Generate", "caption": "Seedance 2.5 at 720P, 9:16 and 10s showing 300 credits on Generate"}], "code": [{"label": "Scene shot prompt with reference binding", "content": "Setting: Dimly lit subway platform at midnight, wet concrete tiles reflecting cool overhead fluorescent tubes.\nCharacter: Marcus, matching @Image1 for facial features and haircut, wearing his olive utility jacket and charcoal crewneck t-shirt.\nAction: Marcus paces along the yellow safety line, glances down at his wrist watch, exhales nervously, and turns toward the empty tunnel tracks.\nCamera: 9:16 vertical medium shot, slight low angle tracking backward smoothly as Marcus walks forward, 50mm lens perspective, subtle atmospheric mist.", "language": "text"}]}, {"id": "question-5", "title": "What causes character drift in AI video, and how do you fix it?", "paragraphs": ["Character drift occurs most frequently when prompts introduce wardrobe variations across shots or when camera trajectories exceed 90-degree rotations. Generative models latch onto clothing textures and color blocks faster than subtle facial geometry. When you change a character's jacket between shots, the model frequently reinterprets their facial age and bone structure to match the new clothing style.", "As detailed in [CawCut's consistency guide](https://www.cawcut.com/help/featured-tutorials/character-consistency-guide), separating facial references from wardrobe references and avoiding extreme occlusions prevents most continuity failures. Furthermore, artistic analyses in [Rutger Tuit's character sheet breakdown](https://rutgertuit.nl/creative/character-sheet) emphasize that testing a character across three quick baseline shots—a wide, a medium, and a close-up—catches identity drift before full scene rendering begins.", "The table below outlines common error states on DramaCanvas and how to resolve them:", "| Error Notice or Artifact | Why It Happens | How to Fix It | |---|---|---| | Facial features morph during camera turns | The model lacks side or rear visual data for that specific angle | Wire a profile or three-quarter card from your master sheet into the Video node before generating. | | Wardrobe changes color or style between cuts | The text prompt omitted the specific garment description | Re-insert the exact wardrobe description into the scene prompt; do not rely on the reference image alone. | | Two characters blend into a single face | Both actors' names and references were submitted without role separation | Refer to actors by visual descriptors (\"the man in the olive jacket\" and \"the woman in the grey coat\") rather than names. | | \"Character voice generation accepts one character card at a time\" | Two character cards were wired into a single Audio node simultaneously | Disconnect extra character cards, leaving only the primary speaking character wired to the Audio node. | | \"This character has no usable voice bound, so a voice task cannot be created\" | The connected character card lacks an assigned voice profile in project assets | Open Project assets, assign a voice profile to the character card, and reconnect the wire to the Audio node. | | \"This image model supports up to 16 reference images.\" | Connected image wires exceed the active image model's capacity | Disconnect extraneous reference wires to bring total upstream inputs within the model limit. | | \"A reference image exceeds this model's file size limit.\" | An uploaded reference file exceeds the 30MB file size ceiling | Compress the source image file before dropping it onto the canvas, then reconnect downstream wires. | | \"Generated, but saving is incomplete\" | Cloud rendering finished, but local network drops delayed saving media | Do not click generate again; wait at least one full minute, then check if the card displays \"Reload resource.\" |", "When camera motion requires complex spatial blocking that exceeds standard prompt steering, you can bypass standard text-to-video limitations. Open the **Director desk** node from the bottom dock to stage 3D virtual actors, lighting positions, and camera tracks before generating your shot. Alternatively, developers can script entire multi-shot scene graphs through the [[link:claude-video-generation-mcp|DramaCanvas MCP server]], automating character reference wiring across hundreds of shots."], "blocks": [{"type": "paragraph", "index": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 1}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 2}, {"type": "table", "index": 3}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 4}]}], "faq": [{"question": "Can you change a character's wardrobe without losing their facial identity?", "answer": "Yes, but you must crop your reference card tightly to the character's head and neck to exclude the previous outfit. When you wire a reference that shows both an old jacket and a face, the video model prioritizes the jacket pattern. By isolating the face and describing the new wardrobe in detail within your text prompt, the model preserves facial identity while rendering the new clothing."}, {"question": "How many reference images should you connect to a video node?", "answer": "Start with 2 to 4 clear reference images. Supplying a front-facing portrait, a three-quarter angle, and a full-body silhouette covers the main angles. Seedance 2.5 accepts up to 30 reference images, so add more angles or outfits only when a shot needs them."}, {"question": "Why did an Audio node reject a connected image or video clip?", "answer": "Audio nodes strictly accept text prompts or exactly one voice-bound character card. They reject incoming video files, standard image stills, and auxiliary audio wires at the input port. To generate dialogue for a character, assign a voice profile to that character card inside Project assets before connecting it to your Audio card."}, {"question": "Do card wiring and character sheet layout consume generation credits?", "answer": "Zero credits are consumed by layout operations. Adding cards, moving nodes across the infinite canvas, cropping reference sheets with Grid split, and dragging connection wires are completely free. Credits are deducted only when you click the upward generate arrow on an individual card, and the exact credit cost is displayed beside the button before submission."}], "media": [], "cta": "/canvas", "closing": ["---", "Maintaining character continuity across an AI video production requires treating character identity as a persistent asset rather than a temporary prompt. By generating a master reference sheet with [[link:gpt-image-guide-2026|GPT Image 2]], saving cropped angles to your project assets, and routing those stills into multi-reference models like [[link:seedance-2-5|Seedance 2.5]] or [[link:kling-ai-guide-2026|Kling 4]], you ensure your cast remains recognizable through every scene transition.", "To begin setting up your production pipeline, open your project board on the [DramaCanvas infinite canvas](https://dramacanva.com), add an Image node to generate your four-view master sheet, and wire your approved stills directly into your first scene's Video node."], "howTo": false};
